        <!-- FAQ -->
        <section id="faq" class="ed-faq scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Frequently asked questions</h2>
          <details open>
            <summary><h3>What is the best trap bar for a home gym?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              For most home gyms, an open trap bar with long sleeves, two handle heights and a built-in jack, such as the REP Fitness Open Trap Bar, is the most versatile choice. If you mainly deadlift and shrug, a closed hex bar such as the Rogue TB-2 or Titan's Rackable Hex Trap Bar does the job and takes less room; on Amazon, Marcy's HTB-6921 and CAP's combo-grip bar are cheaper closed bars with shorter sleeves. In a small room, check the bar's length first.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a trap bar the same as a hex bar?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Yes. Trap bar and hex bar are two names for the same kind of bar: a frame you stand inside, with handles at your sides. Hex bar comes from the hexagonal shape of the classic closed design. Open trap bars remove the back of the frame, so they are no longer hexagons, but they are still sold as hex bars too.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is an open or closed trap bar better?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Neither is better for everyone. Open bars are easier to step in and out of, suit lunges, split squats and carries, and usually include a loading jack, but they are longer, heavier and more expensive. Closed bars are lighter, cheaper, more compact and very rigid, which is all you need if you mainly deadlift and shrug.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>How much does a trap bar weigh?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Usually more than a 45 lb barbell, and it varies widely. The bars on this page weigh from about 46 lb to 65 lb empty, and REP's open bar is 58.4 lb for the frame but 71 to 92 lb once its handles are fitted. Check your bar's real weight in the maker's specifications and use it when you log your lifts.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Will a trap bar fit in my squat rack?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              Only if it is designed to be rackable and its sleeves reach your J-cups. REP says its open bar racks on racks up to 49 inches wide, and Titan and Rogue describe their long bars as fitting most standard racks. Short bars, such as the 59-inch Bells of Steel bar, are floor bars. Measure your rack across the outside of the uprights and compare it with the maker's figure.
            </p>
          </details>
          <details>
            <summary><h3>Is a trap bar deadlift easier on your back?</h3></summary>
            <p class="faq-a">
              It changes where the load goes. Because the weight sits beside you, you can stay more upright, and a 2011 biomechanics study found smaller loading at the lower back and hips and more at the knee than with a straight bar. That does not make it risk-free, and it is not medical advice; if you have a back condition, ask a qualified professional before lifting heavy.
            </p>
          </details>
        </section>

